TypeScript conditional object type inference gotcha: When creating objects conditionally with optional properties, TypeScript may infer `{} | undefined` instead of the proper optional type. 

Example problem:
```typescript
const body = model ? { parts, model } : { parts }
// TypeScript infers: { parts: any; model?: {} | undefined }
// Expected: { parts: any; model?: ModelSelection }
```

Solution: Explicitly type the variable:
```typescript
const body: { parts: any; model?: ModelSelection } = model 
  ? { parts, model } 
  : { parts }
```

Root cause: TypeScript's type inference for conditional expressions with object literals doesn't properly narrow optional property types when one branch omits the property.

Impact: Type errors when passing to functions expecting properly typed optional properties.

Location discovered: packages/core/src/atoms/sessions.ts:156 in promptAsync method.

